# 🖥️ HARDWARE CAPABILITY & ADAPTIVE PERFORMANCE REQUIREMENT

The application must be designed around **hardware capability detection**, not a fixed hardware assumption.

The same application and core architecture must be capable of operating on:

```text
Low-powered / compatibility hardware
        ↓
CPU fallback / lightweight models

Modern integrated GPU systems
        ↓
GPU acceleration where supported

Apple Silicon macOS
        ↓
Metal / CoreML where supported

Windows NVIDIA
        ↓
CUDA / TensorRT where supported

Windows Intel / AMD
        ↓
DirectML or other verified supported backend

Linux NVIDIA
        ↓
CUDA / TensorRT where supported

Linux CPU-only
        ↓
CPU fallback
```

The exact backend must never be assumed. It must be detected and verified against the actual operating system, GPU, driver, runtime, and installed library versions.

## Hardware Detection

At startup, the application must be capable of determining the relevant capabilities of the current system, including where available:

* Operating system.
* CPU architecture.
* CPU core/thread count.
* System RAM.
* GPU vendor.
* GPU model.
* Available GPU memory or relevant memory limits.
* Supported graphics/compute APIs.
* Available AI execution providers.
* Camera capabilities.
* Microphone/audio capabilities.
* Relevant virtual device capabilities.

The result should be used to create an internal hardware capability/performance profile.

The implementation must not hard-code assumptions such as:

```text
"Every computer has CUDA"
"Every Mac has Apple Silicon"
"Every Windows computer has an NVIDIA GPU"
"Every GPU supports the same AI models"
```

## Backend Selection

AI inference must use an abstraction that allows the appropriate execution backend to be selected according to the current machine.

Conceptually:

```text
AI Model
   ↓
Inference Abstraction
   ↓
Available Execution Providers
   ├── CoreML / Metal
   ├── CUDA
   ├── TensorRT
   ├── DirectML
   └── CPU
```

Only execution providers that are actually supported and verified on the current platform may be selected.

If an accelerated backend is unavailable, the application must have an appropriate fallback path whenever the feature can technically operate without that backend.

A missing high-performance GPU must not automatically cause the entire application to fail if a supported lower-performance path exists.

## Adaptive Quality

The application must separate **feature functionality** from **performance level**.

The engine should be capable of selecting or allowing configuration of performance modes such as:

```text
Performance
Balanced
Quality
Custom
```

The actual modes and controls must be implemented only when required by the relevant milestone.

Depending on available hardware, the engine may adjust appropriate parameters such as:

* Camera resolution.
* AI processing resolution.
* Target FPS.
* Detection frequency.
* Tracking frequency.
* Model size/variant.
* Segmentation frequency.
* Optional AI features.
* Voice model configuration.
* Audio processing configuration.

The engine must not unnecessarily run the most computationally expensive model on hardware that cannot reasonably sustain it.

## Feature-Level Scalability

Expensive AI subsystems must be independently scalable.

For example:

```text
Face Swap          ON
Voice Conversion   ON
Lip Sync           ON
Body Tracking      OFF
Garment Overlay    OFF
Advanced BG        OFF
```

on a low-powered system may be valid, while a stronger machine may support:

```text
Face Swap          ON
Voice Conversion   ON
Lip Sync           ON
Body Tracking      ON
Garment Overlay    ON
Advanced BG        ON
```

The application must not require every optional AI feature to execute simultaneously regardless of hardware capability.

## Performance Monitoring

The engine must eventually expose measurable runtime information including, where technically appropriate:

* Current FPS.
* Frame processing latency.
* AI inference latency.
* Audio processing latency.
* CPU utilization.
* GPU utilization.
* Memory usage.
* Dropped frames.
* Audio underruns.
* Queue/buffer depth.
* Model loading time.

Performance decisions must be based on measurements rather than assumptions.

If adaptive performance is introduced, it must react carefully to sustained performance problems rather than causing unstable configuration changes.

