# 🔐 FIREBASE STORAGE, FIRESTORE & ENVIRONMENT CONFIGURATION

Firebase must be used according to the type and size of data being stored.

## Firebase Storage

Use Firebase Storage for large binary assets where appropriate, including:

* Uploaded voice audio files.
* Uploaded source video files when the video is retained.
* Generated/prepared voice-model assets when appropriate.
* Other large user-owned media/model assets introduced by later milestones.

Do not place large binary files directly into Firestore documents.

## Firestore / Realtime Database

Use Firebase database services for metadata and structured information such as:

* User profile information.
* Voice profile names and IDs.
* Voice profile configuration/settings.
* Voice asset references.
* Storage paths.
* Model version information.
* Voice creation/update timestamps.
* Selected/default voice information where appropriate.
* Presets and application configuration.
* Other structured metadata required by later milestones.

The exact Firebase database service must be selected based on the actual requirement of the current milestone rather than using both services unnecessarily.

## Local Cache

Firebase must not be placed in the real-time audio or video processing path.

When a voice model or required asset is available from Firebase:

```text
Firebase Storage
      ↓
Download when required
      ↓
Validate
      ↓
Local cache
      ↓
Load model
      ↓
Real-time inference
```

Once the required voice model is locally available and loaded, real-time voice conversion must operate locally without requiring continuous Firebase communication.

The application must handle unavailable network connectivity gracefully when a previously downloaded voice model is already available locally.

The same principle applies to other AI assets required for real-time processing: download, validate, cache, and prepare them before real-time processing whenever practical.

